{"acceptance_length": 1, "acceptance_lengths": [], "accepted_draft_lengths": [], "benchmark": "math500", "completion": "We are given:\n\n- $ z = 1 + i $\n- $ w = \\dfrac{3z + 1}{5z + 7} $\n\nWe are to find $ |w| $, the **modulus** of $ w $.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Compute $ 3z + 1 $\n\nWe start by computing the numerator:\n\n$$\n3z + 1 = 3(1 + i) + 1 = 3 + 3i + 1 = 4 + 3i\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Compute $ 5z + 7 $\n\nNow compute the denominator:\n\n$$\n5z + 7 = 5(1 + i) + 7 = 5 + 5i + 7 = 12 + 5i\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Write $ w $ as a complex number\n\n$$\nw = \\frac{4 + 3i}{12 + 5i}\n$$\n\nTo find the modulus $ |w| $, we can use the property of complex numbers:\n\n$$\n|w| = \\left| \\frac{a + bi}{c + di} \\right| = \\frac{|a + bi|}{|c + di|}\n$$\n\nSo we compute the moduli of the numerator and denominator.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Compute $ |4 + 3i| $\n\n$$\n|4 + 3i| = \\sqrt{4^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{16 + 9} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Compute $ |12 + 5i| $\n\n$$\n|12 + 5i| = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Compute $ |w| $\n\n$$\n|w| = \\frac{|4 + 3i|}{|12 + 5i|} = \\frac{5}{13}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{13}}\n$$", "draft_calls": 0, "input_ids": [151644, 872, 198, 10061, 400, 89, 284, 220, 16, 21212, 3, 323, 400, 86, 284, 1124, 67, 37018, 90, 18, 89, 10, 16, 15170, 20, 89, 10, 22, 92, 12947, 7379, 400, 91, 86, 80050, 624, 5501, 2874, 3019, 553, 3019, 11, 323, 2182, 697, 1590, 4226, 2878, 1124, 79075, 46391, 151645, 198, 151644, 77091, 198, 151667, 271, 151668, 271], "input_tokens": 60, "method": "native_ar", "output_hash": "27ae291e612d51e3d00122380d6faeba9af53b2aea797994f2a1e7137a68886e", "output_ids": [1654, 525, 2661, 1447, 12, 400, 1147, 284, 220, 16, 488, 600, 24437, 12, 400, 289, 284, 1124, 67, 37018, 90, 18, 89, 488, 220, 16, 15170, 20, 89, 488, 220, 22, 92, 84500, 1654, 525, 311, 1477, 400, 760, 86, 91, 400, 11, 279, 3070, 2593, 19425, 334, 315, 400, 289, 400